SPEC-356b's OWN PATTERNS, RUN AGAINST THIS ARTIFACT (not against the runner's
source -- a code-reading does not discharge this gate)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  $ grep -c 'STEP0C ADMISSIBILITY' spec356a-step0c-fixture.log
  5          <- MUST be non-zero.  PASS

  $ grep -c '<the instrument-defect literal>' spec356a-step0c-fixture.log
  0          <- MUST be zero.      PASS

  The instrument-defect literal is deliberately NOT SPELLED anywhere in this
  file, including in the line that checks for it. Spelling it would make the
  check satisfy itself, and a gate that passes on its own text is exactly the
  vacuous guard this artifact exists to rule out. (The runner had that defect
  when this artifact was first produced: its fixture-path banner spelled the
  literal while merely talking ABOUT it, which made the count 1. The repair was
  in the RUNNER -- rephrase the banner -- never in the check.) The pattern is
  the one SPEC-356b greps: same case, single space, unanchored, colon outside
  the pattern.

  EXIT STATUS: 0 -- the pinned fall-through, `exit "$HARNESS_RC"`. NEVER 1 and
  NEVER 9. A MEASURAND failure is a record, not a failure; the cell STANDS and
  the failure is carried into SPEC-356b's classification walk as an
  admissibility failure that routes the predicate to INDETERMINATE.

  Keeping these bytes character-for-character identical is a constraint on any
  future edit to the runner or to either spec: this artifact is what makes a
  drift mechanically visible instead of silently vacuous.
